{"slug": "wheatstone-bridge-10-applications", "targets": ["function of wheatstone bridge"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">Function of Wheatstone Bridge in Simple Terms</h2><p><strong>The function of Wheatstone bridge</strong> circuits is to measure an unknown resistance, or a small change in resistance, by comparing it with known resistors. At balance the galvanometer reads zero and R1/R2 = R3/Rx, so Rx = R2 R3 / R1. Away from balance the bridge converts a tiny resistance change into a measurable voltage.</p><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">Two Ways the Bridge Works</h3><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Mode</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">How it is used</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Output</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Balanced (null) mode</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Adjust a known arm until the detector reads zero</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Unknown resistance from the ratio</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Unbalanced (deflection) mode</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Keep arms fixed and read the voltage across the middle</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Voltage proportional to resistance change</td></tr></table></div><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">Function in Common Instruments</h3><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Instrument</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Sensing element</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">What the bridge does</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Strain gauge and load cell</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Foil gauges</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Converts strain into a millivolt signal</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">RTD temperature measurement</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Platinum element</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Converts resistance change into voltage</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Pressure transducer</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Gauges on a diaphragm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Gives output proportional to pressure</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Thermal conductivity gas detector</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Heated filaments</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Detects gas by change in cooling</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Cable fault locator</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Faulty cable loop</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Locates the fault distance by resistance ratio</td></tr></table></div><p>Because the detector compares two divider voltages, supply variations largely cancel, which is why the bridge is so accurate.</p>"}